                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Daily Chthonicle: Editor's Edition takes an unconventional approach to supernatural horror by putting the player behind the desk rather than directly in the path of every monster. Developed by Sinister Systems and published by Sometimes You, it combines simulation, role-playing, investigation, and management elements within a gloomy world influenced by Lovecraftian fiction and 1940s noir. You assume control of a supernatural newspaper agency, serving as its boss and editor while attempting to uncover the truth behind increasingly disturbing events. The concept immediately separates the game from conventional horror adventures because information, personnel management, and investigative decisions matter more than direct combat.\n\nThe town surrounding the newspaper is deliberately portrayed as a place where practically anything could be hiding beneath an ordinary appearance. Ghosts, zombies, monsters, murderous shapeshifters, and other unnatural creatures exist alongside unsuspecting citizens. Some threats can even steal bodies or infiltrate society while pretending to be human. This creates an effective atmosphere of uncertainty because reports that initially appear mundane can develop into something considerably more sinister. The horror comes primarily from discovering what is happening rather than watching elaborate cinematic scares, making the experience particularly suitable for players who enjoy mystery and imagination-driven storytelling.\n\nYour primary resources are six seasoned journalists, detectives, and occult specialists. These characters can be assigned to investigate murders, suspicious affairs, supernatural incidents, and other strange occurrences around town. The player therefore operates more like a coordinator of paranormal investigations than a traditional action protagonist. Deciding how to use available personnel becomes an important part of progressing through each developing mystery. There is a satisfying thematic connection between the management mechanics and the setting: every investigation is simultaneously an attempt to protect the town, uncover a supernatural conspiracy, and obtain an interesting story for the newspaper's readers.\n\nOne of the game's most appealing ideas is that its stories are designed to vary between playthroughs. The central supernatural threat is not always identical. A series of violent deaths might eventually reveal a werewolf, while another investigation could expose an extraterrestrial creature capable of stealing bodies. Other scenarios can involve an excavated mummy tormenting unfortunate people, the mysterious return of Jack the Ripper, or something much more cosmic lurking beneath the University. This variety gives the game a pulp-horror anthology quality where each session can feel like another strange edition of the same cursed newspaper.\n\nThe investigative structure benefits from withholding answers until enough information has been gathered. Rather than simply presenting a monster and asking the player to defeat it, the game emphasizes identifying exactly what kind of threat is responsible. That uncertainty fits the Lovecraftian influence particularly well. The player knows that something terrible may be happening, but understanding its nature becomes part of the challenge. The newspaper angle also introduces an entertaining layer of dark humor because horrifying discoveries still need to become stories capable of keeping readers interested.\n\nVisually and mechanically, this is a modest independent production rather than an elaborate modern horror spectacle. Its strength lies in atmosphere, writing, procedural variation, and unusual systems instead of animation-heavy presentation or cinematic production values. The combination of noir imagery and occult subject matter gives it considerable personality, although players accustomed to highly polished interfaces and fast-paced gameplay may find its presentation somewhat austere. Much of the experience involves reading, evaluating situations, issuing assignments, and interpreting results, so patience and an appreciation for text-driven games are important.\n\nReplay value is one of its better qualities because the changing mysteries encourage experimentation beyond a single campaign. The procedural nature of events can produce unexpected combinations of characters, incidents, and supernatural enemies. Inevitably, repeated play can expose familiar patterns, and not every generated situation will be equally memorable. Nevertheless, unpredictability supports the central fantasy of running a newspaper in a town where the next headline could involve anything from an ordinary crime to an ancient cosmic horror.\n\nSteam Deck compatibility requires an important qualification. The game does not currently carry an official Steam Deck Verified or Playable designation on its Steam store listing, and its listed platform support is Windows. This means it should be regarded as officially unverified for Steam Deck rather than guaranteed to work perfectly. Because the game is comparatively lightweight, running it through SteamOS and Proton may be possible, but players should expect potential compatibility or control issues and should not interpret the absence of demanding technology as confirmation of flawless Deck operation.\n\nOverall, Daily Chthonicle: Editor's Edition is memorable because it turns supernatural horror into an administrative and investigative problem. Instead of making another action game about shooting monsters, it asks players to organize reporters, examine disturbing events, uncover hidden creatures, and somehow transform encounters with ancient evil into compelling journalism. Its modest presentation and specialized mechanics will limit its appeal for players seeking action or cinematic horror, but those who enjoy management games, procedural mysteries, occult fiction, and Lovecraftian atmosphere may find its strange combination surprisingly engaging. Its strongest achievement is simply having a personality of its own, something that can be more valuable than technical extravagance in a small independent game.\n\nRating: 7/10",
